WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.450
When it comes to the Netflix original series
Stranger Things, there are a seemingly endless

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:08.400
supply of Easter Eggs and knowing nods to
other movies and pop culture properties.

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:12.540
In fact, it might be faster to list all the
bits of the show that don't actually refer

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:15.020
to something else, rather than those that
do.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:16.020
And that's not all.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:20.230
Sometimes what looks like an Easter Egg — like
Lucas's collection of He-Man action figures

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:24.750
and Maxine's nickname of Mad Max — are actually
just details that come from a show set in

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:25.620
the 1980s.

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:30.940
But in the interests of making your next rewatch
more satisfying, here's a not-anywhere-close-to-complete

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:34.940
list of some of the more overt Easter Eggs
you might've missed in Stranger Things Season

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:35.480
2.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:39.460
And before you get mad and open up your face
with rage like the demogorgon, just remember:

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:40.660
spoilers ahead.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:43.000
Sean Astin never says die

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.040
Sean Astin, who plays Joyce's doomed boyfriend
Bob Newby, got his start as an '80s movie

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.880
icon, playing Mikey in 1985's The Goonies.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.480
That movie, which featured a group of plucky
kids getting in way over their heads on a

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.840
huge adventure, is one of the most obvious
sources of inspiration for Stranger Things.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:03.640
But it's actually one particular line of dialogue
in episode 5, "Dig Dug," that makes Astin's

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:05.900
casting truly work on a list of Easter Eggs.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.920
While helping Joyce, Will, and Mike try to
find Hopper in the maze of tunnels underneath

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:12.880
Hawkins, Indiana, he jokingly asks about where
the map leads.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:15.080
"That's the objective, find the X."

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:15.820
"Yeah?

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:17.040
What's at the X?

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:17.540
Pirate treasure?

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:18.140
Heh heh."

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:19.000
"Bob!"

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.220
And Goonies, as you may recall, revolves around
Mikey and his friends' quest to follow a treasure

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.740
map to find the lost loot of the pirate One-Eyed
Willy.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:31.020
"It's Willie!"

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:32.940
"One-Eyed Willie!"

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.220
Mad about Paul Reiser

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:39.479
While he's probably best remembered these
days for his role as Paul Buchman on the 1990s

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:43.920
sitcom Mad About You, actor Paul Reiser appeared
in Stranger Things as Doctor Owens.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:47.329
He's the new guy running the lab in Hawkins,
who's trying to figure out just what the heck

00:01:47.329 --> 00:01:48.520
is wrong with Will Byers.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:54.659
But back in 1986, Reiser provided one of moviedom's
most memorable bad guy turns in James Cameron's

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:56.740
action-sci-fi sequel Aliens.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.060
He played Burke, a two-faced corporate goon
who sends Ripley and the space marines to

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:06.080
find more xenomorphs, despite knowing that
they'd all probably die horribly in the process.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:10.399
Owens has similarly dubious motivations here,
since neither Joyce nor the viewers can tell

00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:14.110
if the good doctor is really very good at
all — at least not at first.

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:17.360
We find out by the end of the season that
Owens is more Buchman than Burke, though it

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.140
costs us a Bob along the way.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.440
Not much luck if your name begins and ends
with a B in Hawkins, huh?

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:24.000
Staying frosty

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:28.140
Want more proof that Reiser's appearance in
Hawkins is a direct nod to Aliens?

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:33.180
Among the many popular franchises Stranger
Things refers to throughout its second season,

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:34.740
Aliens might be one of the most prominent.

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:39.800
In episode 6, "The Spy," Doctor Owens oversees
a group of gun-toting soldiers who make their

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:43.860
way through the tunnels under Hawkins to try
and confront the shadow monster the kids later

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:45.220
refer to as the mind flayer.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:49.519
He watches on monitors as the soldiers are
ambushed by the demo-dogs who've flooded the

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:50.020
tunnels.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:54.519
That moment takes its cue from a similar scene
in Aliens, when Burke and Ripley watch the

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:57.180
space marines investigate the alien-infested
colony.

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:01.180
Later in the movie, Corporal Hicks talks to
Hudson and Vasquez about the need to be on

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:01.860
high alert:

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:03.660
"We're all in strung out shape.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:06.320
But stay frosty."

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:10.240
So it wasn't too surprising to hear one of
Owens' soldiers say this towards the end of

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:10.740
the episode:

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:15.080
"Alright, stay frosty, boys."

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:17.740
Do Raimi

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:22.580
One of the other major influences that you
can find all over season two of Stranger Things

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:26.520
is the movie Evil Dead, the 1981 horror classic
by Sam Raimi.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:31.180
For starters, when Eleven is home alone in
Hopper's cabin in episode four, "Will the

00:03:31.189 --> 00:03:35.230
Wise," she discovers a secret door in the
floorboards that leads to a hidden basement

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:38.520
that contains secret knowledge about Hopper
and her own past.

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:42.591
The shots that lead her there are pretty visually
similar to the scene where Ash and his friends

00:03:42.591 --> 00:03:46.550
discover the Necronomicon in the basement
in a woodland cabin of their own.

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:50.720
Then there's the abundance of "shaky P.O.V.
cam" shots, which is when a camera zooms along

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:54.040
the ground quickly to show the ominous movements
of an evil force.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:58.299
Raimi used that technique to show the point
of view of the evil spirits stalking Ash and

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:00.780
his doomed friends in all three Evil Dead
movies.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:04.939
Meanwhile, the Duffers bring shaky P.O.V.
cam to Stranger Things when Will sees the

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:08.720
now-memories of the mind flayer, who's tunneling
under Hawkins and spreading his own brand

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:09.220
of evil.

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:13.000
Add the abundance of creepy vines wrapping
themselves around human bodies to hold them

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:16.060
against their will, and the similarities are
impossible to ignore.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:20.240
Still somehow not convinced the Duffers are
paying direct tribute to Raimi with season

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:20.740
two?

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:24.800
Take a look at this Stranger Things promotional
poster — and then look at this original

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:25.920
Evil Dead movie poster.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:29.980
Raimi should maybe get a co-directing credit
for Stranger Things season 2.

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:31.460
The Regan era

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:36.220
Finally, as if throwing Aliens and Evil Dead
into a blender with Goonies weren't enough,

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:39.940
the Duffers and the rest of Stranger Things'
producers threw in a healthy dose of The Exorcist

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:41.800
from 1973 for good measure.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:42.740
We've got it all.

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:46.850
There are young innocents being subjected
to a bunch of pseudo-scientific tests to figure

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:47.860
out what's wrong with them.

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:51.550
Angry mothers demanding that a bunch of quacks
be less terrible at their jobs.

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:55.260
And there are possessed kids being strapped
to a bed for their own good while the demons

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:56.400
are being forced out.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:58.580
The only thing that's missing is projectile
vomit:

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:02.500
Oh, wait.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:04.260
That's in Stranger Things too.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:14.020
Never mind.

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:15.260
Hail to the King

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:19.820
Matt and Ross Duffer, the twin brother creators
of Stranger Things, make no secret of their

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:21.720
appreciation for the works of Stephen King.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.880
So there are plenty of subtle and not-so-subtle
nods to the guy whose work inspired so much

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:26.480
of the show.

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:30.660
For instance, Bob tries to tempt Joyce with
the prospect of relocating her family from

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:31.320
Hawkins, Indiana.

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:33.320
Where does he suggest?

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:37.100
Maine, which just so happens to be the creepy
northeastern state that features prominently

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:38.300
in so much of King's work.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:42.320
Then there's the character of Eleven, whose
psychic powers look pretty reminiscent to

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:45.600
those we see in the 1984 King adaptation Firestarter.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:49.380
A little girl violently busting stuff up using
only her mind?

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:50.340
Yup.

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:54.560
Finally, there's the secret knock that Hopper
uses to let Eleven know that he's home.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:56.340
"(knocking)"

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:04.800
A post on Den of Geek points out that if you
convert the knocks to Morse code, it translates

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:05.880
to the word "ITS."

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:10.520
That sure seems pretty similar to the title
of Stephen King's legendary novel It, which

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:14.520
features a group of outcast friends banding
together to defeat a monster that's terrorizing

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:15.020
their town.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:18.940
The fact that actor Finn Wolfhard, who plays
Mike on Stranger Things, appears as Richie

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:23.620
Tozier in the 2017 big screen adaptation of
It, potentially making Hopper's knock one

00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:26.160
of the best-hidden Easter Eggs in the whole
series.

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:27.400
He's back!

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:28.900
In Easter Egg form

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:33.800
Near the end of Episode 4, "Will the Wise,"
Dustin comes home to spend time with his rapidly

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:35.380
growing pet demon, Dart.

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:38.580
That's when he's shocked to discover that
Dart has broken out of his cage and found

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:41.840
a new favorite cuisine: Dustin's cat, Mews.

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:43.900
What could this be a reference to?

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:44.960
You guessed it:

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:46.720
"Hey Willie!"

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:48.400
"Alf!"

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:49.680
"Willie!"

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:51.520
"Alf!"

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:54.200
"Willie!"

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:59.940
That's right, ALF — that wacky puppet from
the planet Melmac who had his own sitcom in

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:03.240
the late 1980s — also liked to feast on
felines.

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:05.100
"Any last meows?

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:08.340
Nope?

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:10.280
Then I'll see you in a few days."

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:15.400
Is it possible that this Easter Egg is actually
just more of a coincidence than an intentional

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:15.900
one?

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:17.100
Yeah, probably.

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:21.460
But is it way more fun to imagine that the
Duffers and the rest of their team spent hours

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:25.750
trying to think of a way to cram an obscure
reference to ALF into their hit TV show and

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:28.040
that only super ALF nerds would recognize?

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:29.040
Absolutely.

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:30.520
Thanks for watching!

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:32.640
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:35.520
Plus check out all this other cool stuff we
know you'll love too.

